@@s001 Clustering: k-means, mean shift, agglomerative
@@s002 dato:group of c1,due:group of c2
@@s003 Six numbers and k = 2 centres, placed (badly) at 1 and 2.
@@s004 <b>Assign</b>: each point goes to the nearest centre. Only the 1 is nearer to c1; all the others to c2.
@@s005 <b>Update</b>: each centre goes to the mean of its group. c1 = 1, c2 = (2 + 3 + 10 + 11 + 12) / 5 = <b>7.6</b>.
@@s006 Assign again: 2 and 3 are now nearer to c1 (1) than to c2 (7.6).
@@s007 Update: c1 = <b>2</b>, c2 = <b>11</b>.
@@s008 Assign again: no point changes group, so the centres no longer move: <b>convergence</b>. Final inertia: 1 + 0 + 1 + 1 + 0 + 1 = 4.
@@s009 ora:window of radius h,no:the point that moves
@@s010 dato:cluster 1,due:cluster 2,ora:cluster 3
@@s011 dato:feature
@@s012 Clustering: k-means, mean shift, agglomerative
@@s013 Lecture 12 and the exercise of week 13 (<i>Clustering</i>, on the sequences of poses). <b>Unsupervised</b> learning: no labels, one looks for groups of similar data. Three methods with three different ideas of "group". Exam question <b>Q11</b>. DW chapter 23.
@@s014 K-means
@@s015 The algorithm on 6 numbers: [1, 2, 3, 10, 11, 12], k = 2, initial centres 1 and 2
@@s016 assign: each point to the <mark class="ora">nearest centre</mark> → [1] | [2, 3, 10, 11, 12]
@@s017 The 3 and the points on the right are nearer to centre 2 than to centre 1.
@@s018 update: each centre = <mark class="ok">mean</mark> of its group → 1 and 7.6
@@s019 assign again → [1, 2, 3] | [10, 11, 12]; update → <mark class="ok">2 and 11</mark>
@@s020 Now the 2 and the 3 are nearer to 1 than to 7.6.
@@s021 assign again → nothing changes ⇒ <mark class="ok">convergence</mark>
@@s022 Three iterations (verified). Each step lowers the sum of the squared distances from the centres (the <b>inertia</b>), so the algorithm always stops; but it can stop in a local minimum if the initial centres are unlucky: that is why sklearn restarts several times (<code>n_init</code>).
@@s023 K-means, one iteration at a time
@@s024 inertia
@@s025 <b>Elbow method</b>, on 300 points generated with 3 groups (<code>make_blobs</code>, <code>KMeans</code>): the inertia always falls as k increases (with k = N it is 0), but up to 3 it falls a lot and soon after little. The "elbow" suggests k = 3. Pros: simple and fast. Limits: k must be chosen; round groups of similar size; sensitive to the scale of the features and to outliers.
@@s026 The warning on Windows
@@s027 With Anaconda on Windows <code>KMeans</code> prints "KMeans is known to have a memory leak on Windows with MKL…". It is not an error: the results are right. To make it disappear: the environment variable <code>OMP_NUM_THREADS=2</code> before importing sklearn.
@@s028 Mean shift
@@s029 Climbing towards the densest point
@@s030 window of radius <mark class="ora">h</mark> (bandwidth) around a point
@@s031 Only the neighbourhood is looked at.
@@s032 move the point to the <mark class="ok">weighted mean</mark> of the neighbours (the mean shift vector m(x))
@@s033 The mean lies on the side where there are more points: the point climbs towards the densest zone (a maximum of the density).
@@s034 repeat until it no longer moves; points that reach the same peak = <mark class="due">same cluster</mark>
@@s035 The number of clusters is not chosen: it comes out by itself. One chooses h.
@@s036 bandwidth h
@@s037 clusters found
@@s038 The same 300 points, <code>MeanShift(bandwidth=h)</code>. h too small: every small crowding becomes a cluster. h too large: everything merges. h has the same role as k, put in another way (Task 11).
@@s039 Climbing towards the density, and the role of h
@@s040 Agglomerative clustering
@@s041 From the bottom upwards
@@s042 at the start every point is a cluster
@@s043 N clusters.
@@s044 "Near" depends on the <b>linkage</b>: single (the two nearest points), complete (the farthest), average, <b>ward</b> (the merge that increases the internal variance the least).
@@s045 the <mark class="ok">dendrogram</mark> records every merge and at what distance
@@s046 Cutting the tree at a height gives the number of clusters: a long jump before a merge indicates well-separated groups.
@@s047 On the same data with 3 clusters: ward gives groups of 105, 100 and 95 points; single linkage 199, 1 and 100: an isolated point becomes a cluster on its own and two nearby groups "chain" together. The linkage changes the result.
@@s048 Ward against single linkage
